(function () {
  window.CCA = window.CCA || {};
  window.CCA.lessonsNew = window.CCA.lessonsNew || [];
  window.CCA.scenarios = window.CCA.scenarios || {};
  window.CCA.why = window.CCA.why || {};
  window.CCA.questions = window.CCA.questions || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }

  window.CCA.lessonsNew.push(
    {
      "id": "eval-1", "n": 1, "domain": "eval",
      "title": "Turning vague goals into measurable evaluation metrics",
      "body": "Evaluation starts with success criteria, not with tooling. Anthropic's guidance says good criteria are specific, measurable, achievable and relevant. \"The assistant should be good\" is not a criterion; \"fewer than a stated share of outputs flagged by a content classifier across a fixed prompt set\" is. Even hazy topics such as safety can be quantified this way.\n\nMost applications need several dimensions at once: task fidelity, consistency, tone, privacy preservation, context use, latency and cost. The architect's job is to choose which dimensions gate a release, which are tracked as guardrails, and what trade-offs are acceptable. A feature where a fabricated citation is costly needs a faithfulness gate, not just a satisfaction score.\n\nSecurity is its own dimension. For agents that read external content, a useful metric is how often injected instructions change the agent's actions in adversarial test cases. Report results per category, because an aggregate score can hide a rare but important failure class. For cost, compare cost per successfully completed task, including retries and output tokens, rather than list price alone.",
      "points": [
        "Good success criteria are specific, measurable, achievable and relevant to the application's purpose.",
        "Use several dimensions together: quality, latency, cost, safety and security, rather than accuracy alone.",
        "Turn hazy goals such as safety into quantified targets on a fixed test set.",
        "Pick release-gate metrics from the costliest failure mode, and track the rest as guardrails.",
        "Report per-category results so an aggregate score cannot hide a weak segment.",
        "For latency, match the metric to the experience: time to first token matters when streaming, and tail percentiles matter more than means."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Treating a qualitative goal as a metric without defining how it will be scored.",
        "Optimising a single dimension, usually accuracy, and discovering the cost or latency problem after launch.",
        "Comparing models on list price per token instead of cost per completed task.",
        "Using adoption or volume figures as if they measured quality."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-latency"
      ],
      "code": "Goal            : trustworthy clause answers\nGate (release)  : unsupported-citation rate on held-out set <= target\nGuardrails      : answer rate, p95 latency, cost per task\nSafety/security : injected-instruction success rate = 0 on red-team set\nReport          : per category, not only overall"
    },
    {
      "id": "eval-2", "n": 2, "domain": "eval",
      "title": "Building evaluation datasets and choosing graders",
      "body": "An evaluation set should mirror the real task distribution, including edge cases such as ambiguous, unanswerable, adversarial or badly formatted inputs. Anthropic's guidance also says to automate grading where possible and to prefer more test cases with slightly noisier automated grading over a few hand-graded ones. Claude can help generate extra cases from a baseline set, but a person should review them.\n\nChoose the fastest, most reliable and most scalable grader that fits the criterion. Code-based grading (exact or string match) is fastest and most reliable but lacks nuance. Human grading is the most flexible but slow and expensive. LLM-based grading is fast, flexible and scalable for complex judgement, but should be tested for reliability against human labels before you scale it.\n\nFor LLM graders, write a detailed rubric, require a constrained verdict (correct or incorrect, or a 1 to 5 scale), and consider letting the grader reason before it scores. A mixed design is normal: code checks for format and fields, an LLM grader for tone or faithfulness, and periodic human review to calibrate. Keep a held-out set that you never tune against, and run a regression suite on every change.",
      "points": [
        "Sample the live distribution and deliberately add edge cases; do not use only clean examples.",
        "Match the grader to the criterion: code for exact outputs, LLM for judgement, humans for calibration.",
        "Validate an LLM grader against human labels before trusting it at scale.",
        "Give LLM graders a detailed rubric and a constrained verdict format.",
        "Keep a held-out set separate from the cases used for prompt tuning to detect overfitting.",
        "Run a regression suite automatically on every prompt, model or retrieval change."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Tuning a prompt against the same cases used to report success.",
        "Using an LLM grader with no rubric and then aggregating free-text opinions.",
        "Insisting on human grading for everything, which limits volume and iteration speed.",
        "Using only synthetic cases that nobody has checked against real traffic."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"
      ],
      "code": "Criterion            Grader\nLabel is exact       code: output == expected\nKey phrase present   code: phrase in output\nTone / faithfulness  LLM grader + rubric (validated)\nCalibration          human sample each release"
    },
    {
      "id": "eval-3", "n": 3, "domain": "eval",
      "title": "A/B testing and disciplined iterative improvement",
      "body": "Improvement follows a loop: define criteria, build cases, test a prompt, refine, and confirm on held-out evals before shipping. Discipline matters more than cleverness. Anthropic's documentation lists A/B testing, comparing against a baseline model or earlier version, as one quantitative method, but it does not publish a step-by-step experiment design. The A/B guidance below is therefore general experimentation practice, applied to Claude systems. Change one variable at a time so you know what helped, hold the model and the eval set constant when comparing prompts, and add every fixed failure to the regression suite so it stays fixed.\n\nOffline evals come first because they are cheap and safe. Online experiments then answer questions offline sets cannot, such as how real users and real inputs respond. For a fair A/B test, assign comparable traffic to variants concurrently and at random, decide the primary metric before you look at results, and track guardrail metrics such as latency, cost per task and safety flags, because a gain in one dimension can hide a loss in another.\n\nTreat small differences on small sets with caution: a two-point gain on thirty cases may be noise, so add cases or repeat runs. When a change creates a trade-off, such as fewer unsupported claims but more refusals of answerable questions, quantify both sides and tune against targets rather than choosing one metric to celebrate. Roll out in stages, with a small share of traffic first and a rollback ready.",
      "points": [
        "Change one variable at a time and keep the eval set and model fixed when comparing prompts.",
        "Run offline regression evals first, then a canary or A/B test on live traffic.",
        "Randomise concurrently across comparable traffic and fix the primary metric in advance.",
        "Track guardrail metrics (latency, cost, safety) so a quality gain cannot hide a regression elsewhere.",
        "Treat small differences on small samples as possibly noise.",
        "Add each fixed failure to the regression set and rerun the whole set before release."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Running variant A one week and variant B the next, which confounds the change with time.",
        "Changing prompt, retrieval and model together, then being unable to attribute the result.",
        "Retesting only the reported failures and missing regressions elsewhere.",
        "Declaring victory on the primary metric while a guardrail metric degrades."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests",
        "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents"
      ],
      "code": "Loop: change ONE thing -> run regression set -> compare\n      -> canary small share -> A/B on live traffic\n      -> check guardrails -> expand or roll back"
    },
    {
      "id": "eval-4", "n": 4, "domain": "eval",
      "title": "Diagnosing prompt failures, hallucinations and model mismatch",
      "body": "Diagnosis begins with categorising failures by cause instead of rewriting the prompt at random. Typical causes are retrieval that never supplied the needed content, instructions that are ambiguous or conflicting, a missing format constraint, output truncated at the token limit, and a task that exceeds what the chosen model can do. Controlled changes on the eval set, such as removing or adding rules in steps, show which instruction is ignored or in conflict.\n\nFor hallucination, first check whether the relevant source was actually in context. If it was missing, fix retrieval; if it was present, tighten grounding. Anthropic documents several techniques: let the model say it does not know, ask for word-for-word quotes before answering on long documents, require citations and retract unsupported claims, restrict the model to the provided documents, and compare repeated runs, since inconsistency can signal fabrication. These reduce hallucination but do not eliminate it.\n\nA model mismatch looks different: many prompt iterations produce little gain on a reasoning-heavy slice. Then test a more capable model, or route only the hard cases to it. Check stop_reason when structured output breaks mid-object, because truncation at max_tokens is a common cause. Also check that the eval set resembles production; good offline scores with user complaints often mean the set or the rubric is not representative.",
      "points": [
        "Categorise failures by cause (retrieval, instruction, format, truncation, capability) before changing anything.",
        "Check whether the needed source was in context to separate retrieval faults from generation faults.",
        "Use documented grounding techniques: permit uncertainty, quote first, cite and verify, restrict to provided documents.",
        "Compare repeated runs to flag unstable answers that may be fabricated.",
        "Little gain after many prompt iterations on a hard slice suggests model mismatch.",
        "Inspect stop_reason when outputs are cut off or fail to parse."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Switching to a larger model before checking retrieval or the prompt.",
        "Adding a rule per failing case until the prompt becomes contradictory.",
        "Raising output limits blindly instead of checking whether truncation occurred.",
        "Assuming a hallucination technique removes the risk entirely, so critical claims need no validation."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/handling-stop-reasons"
      ],
      "code": "Symptom                         First check\nFact not in documents           was passage retrieved?\nParse error, cut mid-object     stop_reason == max_tokens?\nInconsistent across runs        repeat N times, compare\nNo gain after many edits        test a stronger model on the slice"
    },
    {
      "id": "eval-5", "n": 5, "domain": "eval",
      "title": "Optimising tokens, latency and cost without losing quality",
      "body": "Optimise after you know what good looks like. Anthropic's latency guidance says to engineer a prompt that works well first, then apply latency measures, because premature optimisation can hide what top performance looks like. After each change, rerun the eval set so a saving does not silently cost quality.\n\nThe main levers are well documented. Choose an appropriately sized model or effort level for each step instead of using the largest model everywhere. Trim redundant prompt text and ask for concise output; sentence or paragraph limits work better than exact word counts, and max_tokens is a blunt hard stop. Stream responses to improve perceived latency for interactive use. Use prompt caching for large static prefixes, with static content placed first and anything that changes per request after the cached portion. Check the usage fields: if cache creation and cache read counts are both zero, the prompt was not cached, for instance because it was below the minimum length.\n\nMatch workload to API shape. The Message Batches API is asynchronous, typically cheaper, and suited to bulk work where nobody is waiting, such as archive processing or large evaluation runs. Interactive requests need synchronous calls. Routing routine cases to a smaller model and escalating ambiguous ones is a common cost-performance design, but measure both paths.",
      "points": [
        "Reach target quality first, then optimise, and re-evaluate after every optimisation.",
        "Route by task difficulty: smaller or lower-effort settings for routine steps, stronger ones for hard cases.",
        "Trim prompts, request concise output with sentence limits, and use max_tokens as a safety cap, not a style control.",
        "Put static content at the start of the prompt for caching and verify hits in the usage fields.",
        "Stream for perceived latency; batch for non-interactive bulk work.",
        "Compare designs on cost per successful task and tail latency, not on token price alone."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Optimising latency before the feature meets its quality target.",
        "Placing per-request data or timestamps before the cached content, so the prefix never matches.",
        "Using the Batches API for a request a user is waiting on.",
        "Capping max_tokens tightly and truncating answers mid-sentence."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-latency",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing"
      ],
      "code": "Interactive step : stream, small/low-effort model, cached prefix\nHard cases       : escalate to stronger model, measure both paths\nBulk / nightly   : Message Batches API\nVerify           : usage.cache_read_input_tokens > 0 ; eval set unchanged"
    },
    {
      "id": "eval-6", "n": 6, "domain": "eval",
      "title": "Monitoring Claude systems with logging and observability",
      "body": "Offline evals tell you about the past; monitoring tells you what production is doing now. Log enough per request to diagnose later: prompt version, model ID, token usage including cache counts, stop_reason, latency, error and retry status, and for agents a trace of each step with tool calls, inputs, outputs and timing, correlated by request. Without prompt and model versions on each record, a quality change cannot be tied to a release.\n\nMonitor quality as well as health. A successful HTTP response says nothing about whether the answer was good, so score a sample of live outputs against the same rubric used offline, broken down by category, and watch the trend. Use percentiles for latency, because averages hide tail behaviour and peak-hour effects. Track cost per task, and use usage reporting broken down by model, workspace or key to localise cost changes; Anthropic offers a Usage and Cost API for organisations, and several observability platforms integrate with Claude usage.\n\nAlert on sustained movement beyond a baseline band rather than on every single failure, to avoid alert fatigue. Logs may contain personal or regulated data, so decide with Privacy, Legal or the Data Privacy Officer what to log, how to redact it and how long to keep it.",
      "points": [
        "Log prompt version, model ID, token and cache usage, stop_reason, latency and errors on every request.",
        "Trace each step of an agent run, including tool calls, so slow or wrong results can be localised.",
        "Score sampled live outputs with the offline rubric, because HTTP success does not show quality.",
        "Use latency percentiles and cost per task, segmented by category, time and model.",
        "Alert on sustained deviation from a baseline, not on isolated failures.",
        "Agree log content, redaction and retention with privacy and compliance owners."
      ],
      "traps": [
        "Watching only error rates and uptime while answer quality drifts.",
        "Reporting average latency and missing tail or peak-hour slowdowns.",
        "Logging raw personal data indefinitely because it might be useful later.",
        "Failing to record the prompt version, so a regression cannot be attributed."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/manage-claude/usage-cost-api",
        "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"
      ],
      "code": "Per request : prompt_ver, model, in/out tokens, cache r/w,\n              stop_reason, latency, error, retries\nAgent       : trace per step (tool, input, output, ms)\nQuality     : sample N% -> rubric score by category\nAlerts      : p95 latency, cost/task, score band, error rate"
    }
  );

  window.CCA.scenarios.scEV1 = { "title": "Clause-answer assistant for a knowledge team", "text": "A professional-services knowledge team is preparing to launch a Claude assistant that answers questions about contract clauses by retrieving passages from an internal clause library. A fabricated or unsupported clause citation is far worse than a refusal. So far quality has been judged by reading about 40 answers and agreeing they looked right. The team has a few weeks to produce evidence for a rollout decision and expects to iterate on prompts and retrieval settings." };
  window.CCA.scenarios.scEV2 = { "title": "High-volume ticket classification and summary pipeline", "text": "A support operations team runs a pipeline that classifies each incoming ticket and then writes a short summary for the assigned agent. Volume is high and most tickets are routine. A nightly job also re-classifies the previous day's archive. Today every step uses the largest model with a long fixed prompt. Finance wants lower cost, agents need summaries within a few seconds of arrival, and the team has a labelled set of 500 tickets." };

  window.CCA.questions.push(
    S("ev001", "eval", "A team defines success for a summarisation feature as \"good summaries\". Which revision turns this into a usable evaluation metric?", ["Ask a few colleagues whether the summaries feel good before release", "Count summaries generated per day and treat volume as a quality signal", "Set a rubric-scored faithfulness target on a fixed test set", "Compare each summary's length with the longest the model has produced"], 2, "Success criteria should be specific and measurable. A rubric-scored rate on a fixed set with a target can be repeated and compared across versions."),
    S("ev002", "eval", "An internal HR assistant is judged only on answer accuracy. Which gap in its metric set matters most before wider rollout?", ["Nothing tracks latency, cost or safety next to accuracy", "No tracking of how often employees open the tool each week", "No leaderboard position for the underlying model on public benchmarks", "No record of which engineer wrote the system prompt"], 0, "Most applications need multidimensional evaluation. Accuracy alone can hide a latency, cost or safety problem that blocks adoption."),
    S("ev003", "eval", "A fabricated citation is the costliest failure here. Which metric should act as a release gate?", ["Average response length across a sample of recent answers", "Overall satisfaction from a post-chat pop-up for users", "Median time to the first streamed token for each reply", "Unsupported-citation rate on a held-out set"], 3, "The release gate should measure the costliest failure directly. Unsupported citations on held-out cases test exactly that risk.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev004", "eval", "A team needs a measurable safety criterion for a public chatbot. Which version is best specified?", ["Outputs should be safe and appropriate for all audiences", "Fewer than a stated share of outputs flagged by a safety classifier on a fixed prompt set", "The red team agreed the bot seemed robust after one informal session of testing", "The system prompt contains a long section about safe and polite behaviour for the bot"], 1, "Anthropic's guidance shows even hazy goals such as safety can be quantified, for example a flagged-output rate over many trials."),
    S("ev005", "eval", "An interface streams answers to users as they are generated. Which latency metric best reflects how responsive it feels?", ["Total time to the last token for the longest answers", "Time to first token, tracked at a high percentile of requests", "Mean tokens per second averaged across all requests", "Time the client spends building each request body before sending it"], 1, "With streaming, perceived responsiveness is driven by how soon output starts, and tail percentiles show what slower users experience."),
    S("ev006", "eval", "An agent reads external web pages through a tool. Which metric addresses its security dimension specifically?", ["Task completion rate on benign requests across the whole test set", "Cost per completed task measured across the busiest hour of the day", "Token usage per tool call, averaged across all agent runs in the week", "Success rate of injected instructions in fetched content"], 3, "Security needs its own adversarial metric. Benign completion, cost and token counts cannot show whether injected content steers the agent."),
    S("ev007", "eval", "Overall accuracy is 94% on a test set, but a rare complaint category that the set barely covers makes up a meaningful slice of production traffic. What is the best conclusion?", ["Report per-category results and add coverage for the rare category", "94% is sufficient because rare categories average out over time", "Raise the overall target to 99% and leave the categories unexamined until later", "Replace accuracy with latency as the headline metric"], 0, "Aggregates can hide weak segments. Per-category reporting and better coverage expose and measure the risk."),
    M("ev008", "eval", 2, "Which TWO are properties of a well-formed success criterion for an LLM feature?", ["It is phrased qualitatively so reviewers can interpret it flexibly", "It is specific to the task being evaluated", "It demands a perfect score so that no error is ever tolerated", "It is measurable, by numbers or a consistently applied scale", "It is defined only after launch, so real traffic can shape it"], [1, 3], "Anthropic's guidance lists specific, measurable, achievable and relevant. Flexible wording, perfection targets and late definition all undermine testing."),
    S("ev009", "eval", "Two model configurations score similarly on quality. Which cost comparison is most decision-useful?", ["Listed input price per million tokens for each candidate model", "Total invoice from the first month of pilot traffic only", "Cost per completed task, including retries and output", "Number of API calls made per user session in production"], 2, "Cost per completed task captures prompt size, output length and retries together. List price alone ignores how much each configuration actually uses."),
    S("ev010", "eval", "A team builds its first eval set for a ticket classifier. Which source mix best matches how the system will be used?", ["Real or realistic tickets across the live distribution, plus deliberately added rare edge cases", "Only clean, textbook examples written by the prompt authors, with no messy real tickets", "Only the hardest cases found so far, with no routine tickets from typical traffic", "Only the examples already shown as few-shot examples in the prompt, reused as the test set"], 0, "Evals should mirror the real task distribution and include edge cases. Clean-only, hard-only or prompt-copied sets give a distorted or inflated picture."),
    S("ev011", "eval", "The team has 40 hand-read answers. How should it build the eval set behind the rollout decision?", ["Keep the 40, since reading them has already shown the quality level", "Have the model write questions and answers from the library and use them without any human review", "Grow the set with representative questions, edge cases such as conflicting clauses, and expert-checked references", "Use only the questions that earlier prompt versions already answered correctly, to keep scores stable"], 2, "A rollout decision needs more volume, realistic coverage and trustworthy references. Unreviewed synthetic data or filtered easy cases would mislead.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev012", "eval", "A classifier must output exactly one of five labels. Which grading method is most appropriate?", ["Human reviewers reading every output", "Code-based exact match against the one expected label", "An LLM grader scoring tone on a 1 to 5 scale", "Cosine similarity between embeddings of the labels"], 1, "For exact-output tasks, code-based grading is the fastest, most reliable and most scalable option."),
    S("ev013", "eval", "The team wants to score at scale whether each answer is supported by its cited passage, which needs some judgement. Which approach fits best?", ["Exact string match between the answer and the cited passage text", "Human grading of every answer by a reviewer in each release cycle", "A keyword check for the clause title anywhere in the answer text", "An LLM grader with a clear rubric, validated on a human-labelled sample"], 3, "Support judgement needs nuance that exact matching lacks, while human grading does not scale. An LLM grader is suitable once its reliability is tested.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev014", "eval", "An LLM grader returns long free-text opinions that are hard to aggregate. Which change improves it most?", ["Remove the rubric so the grader can judge each answer holistically and in its own words", "Use the same model as the one being graded, so that the grading styles match closely", "Ask for longer critiques so that numeric scores can be inferred from them later on", "Give a detailed rubric and require a constrained final verdict, such as correct or incorrect"], 3, "Anthropic's grading tips call for detailed rubrics and empirical, constrained outputs so results can be compared and aggregated."),
    S("ev015", "eval", "A team can hand-grade 30 outputs per week, or auto-grade several hundred per run with slightly noisier signal. For iterative prompt work, which is the better default?", ["Hand-grade the 30, since automated grading cannot be trusted at all", "Automate grading, and spot-check with humans", "Alternate randomly between the two methods each week", "Grade only the failures, as passing cases carry no information"], 1, "Anthropic's guidance favours more cases with automated grading over fewer hand-graded ones. Human spot checks calibrate the automation."),
    S("ev016", "eval", "After weeks of tuning against 80 cases, a prompt scores 97% on those same cases. What is the main risk to address?", ["Latency will rise sharply, so a dedicated streaming test is needed", "The model has memorised the cases, which forces fine-tuning", "The prompt is overfit, so confirm on a held-out set", "Rate limits will be exceeded on the next evaluation run"], 2, "Tuning against the same cases that report success inflates results. A held-out set not used in tuning gives an honest estimate."),
    M("ev017", "eval", 2, "Which TWO choices form a sensible mixed-methods evaluation design?", ["Human grading of the full set after every small prompt edit", "Code-graded checks for format and fields, plus LLM grading of tone against a rubric", "Public benchmark scores alone, since they are independent of your data", "LLM grading for every criterion, with the rubric never compared to human judgement", "Periodic human review of a sample to calibrate the automated graders"], [1, 4], "Different criteria suit different graders, and human samples keep automated graders calibrated. The other options lack calibration, scale poorly or ignore your task."),
    S("ev018", "eval", "A team edits its prompt weekly and keeps seeing old failures return. Which framework element addresses this?", ["A regression suite of past failures that runs automatically on each change", "A larger model for production traffic", "A manual checklist reviewed at the monthly meeting", "A longer system prompt that lists every past bug and the fix applied for each"], 0, "Automated regression tests turn fixed failures into permanent checks, so a change that reintroduces one is caught before release."),
    S("ev019", "eval", "Two prompt variants will be compared in production. Which design gives the most trustworthy comparison?", ["Run variant A for one week, then variant B the following week, and compare the totals", "Let each user choose their preferred variant in settings and compare the resulting scores", "Randomly assign comparable traffic to each variant concurrently, judged on a predefined metric", "Show both variants to reviewers who know which is which and ask them to pick a winner"], 2, "Concurrent random assignment removes time and selection effects, and a predefined metric prevents choosing the result after the fact. This is general experimentation practice, not an Anthropic-specific rule."),
    S("ev020", "eval", "A new prompt version is ready. What is the most sensible first step before exposing real users?", ["Run old and new versions offline on the regression set, then canary a small share", "Switch all traffic and watch for complaints", "Release to internal staff for a full month without recording any usage metrics or results", "Ask the model whether the new prompt is better"], 0, "Offline evals are cheap and safe, and a canary limits exposure while production metrics are checked. Staged rollout is general release practice rather than Anthropic guidance."),
    S("ev021", "eval", "The team will compare two chunk sizes for retrieval. Which method is best?", ["Change chunking, the prompt and the model together so the best mix emerges quickly", "Score each setting on a different question set to widen coverage", "Pick the setting that wins on the few questions the team remembers best", "Vary only the chunking, keep prompt and model fixed, and score both on the same set"], 3, "Changing one variable and holding everything else constant allows attribution of any difference to that variable.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev022", "eval", "A new variant beats the old one by two points on 30 test cases. What is the sound next step?", ["Declare the win and roll it out to all users straight away", "Add cases or repeat runs; the gap may be noise", "Discard it, because small margins are not worth pursuing", "Average its score with the old variant's and keep both"], 1, "Small differences on small samples may be noise, a general statistical point. More cases or repeated runs give a basis for a decision."),
    M("ev023", "eval", 3, "Which THREE should an A/B test of a cheaper model configuration track beyond the primary quality metric?", ["Public leaderboard rank of the cheaper model", "Latency percentiles", "Total number of prompt edits made during the test", "Cost per completed task", "Rate of safety flags or escalations", "Satisfaction of the team that built the old configuration"], [1, 3, 4], "Guardrail metrics for latency, cost and safety show whether a gain in one dimension hides a loss in another, which is general experimentation practice. The other options do not measure the system's behaviour."),
    S("ev024", "eval", "After one iteration, faithfulness improved but the refusal rate on answerable questions also rose. How should the team treat this?", ["Ship it, since faithfulness is the release gate and the extra refusals are an acceptable cost", "Treat it as a trade-off: add an answer-rate metric and tune until both meet targets", "Revert permanently, since any rise in refusals is unacceptable", "Drop the refused questions from the test set so the metric looks clean"], 1, "A change that improves one dimension and harms another should be quantified on both and tuned against explicit targets.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev025", "eval", "A prompt revision fixes five reported failures. Which step best protects against hidden regressions?", ["Deploy it right away, since the reported failures are the only ones that matter", "Retest only the five failures that were reported and confirm that they now pass", "Ask users to report any new problems that they notice during the following week", "Add the five failures to the regression set and rerun the full set before release"], 3, "Rerunning the full set catches regressions elsewhere, and adding the failures keeps them from returning."),
    S("ev026", "eval", "A new model version looks better offline. Which rollout approach best manages unknown production differences?", ["Send a small share of live traffic to it and watch metrics", "Replace the old model at once, because offline evals have already settled the question", "Run it only on weekends, when traffic is lower and risk is smaller", "Start with the most complex users, who will give the best feedback"], 0, "A staged rollout with defined metrics limits impact and gives production evidence, as general release practice. Offline results cannot cover every real input."),
    S("ev027", "eval", "A team changes the prompt, the retrieval depth and the model in one release, and quality improves. What is the main weakness of this approach?", ["It proves that the prompt was the weak point in the pipeline all along", "It guarantees that each of the three changes contributed to the gain", "The team cannot tell which change helped or hurt", "It prevents latency from being measured"], 2, "Bundled changes are confounded. Changing one variable at a time is standard experimentation practice and allows attribution and safe iteration."),
    S("ev028", "eval", "Answers are fluent but state facts that are absent from the provided documents. What is the best first diagnostic step?", ["Check whether the relevant passage was retrieved", "Switch to a larger model for all requests in the pipeline", "Increase max_tokens so that answers have more room", "Reduce the number of examples in the prompt to simplify it"], 0, "Separating retrieval faults from generation faults directs the fix. Changing model or limits first skips the diagnosis."),
    M("ev029", "eval", 2, "Which TWO techniques are documented ways to reduce hallucination on long-document tasks?", ["Remove citations to keep output short", "Tell the model to use general knowledge when the documents are silent", "Ask for word-for-word quotes first, then answer from them", "Lengthen answers so that an occasional error is diluted", "Allow the model to say it does not know"], [2, 4], "Anthropic's guidance recommends permitting uncertainty and grounding answers in extracted quotes. Allowing general knowledge, dropping citations or padding do not reduce unsupported claims."),
    S("ev030", "eval", "The assistant cites a clause that does not appear anywhere in the retrieved context. Which fix addresses the unsupported citation itself?", ["Re-index the library with smaller chunks and rebuild the retrieval step for all documents", "Increase the number of retrieved passages passed to the model to give it more context", "Require exact-quote clause citations and verify each one in code, rejecting unsupported claims", "Switch the grader to a cheaper model to lower the cost of every evaluation run on the set"], 2, "The clause was not retrieved, so this is a generation fault. Quote-based citation with verification makes unsupported claims detectable and rejectable.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev031", "eval", "A small, cheap model fails a reasoning-heavy slice of a task, and 20 prompt iterations change little. What is the best diagnosis?", ["The eval set is too small, so keep iterating on the prompt for longer", "Likely a model capability mismatch; test a stronger model", "The temperature is too high for this kind of reasoning task", "Add many more few-shot examples until the slice finally passes"], 1, "Persistent failure despite many prompt changes suggests the model is the limiting factor. Routing only the hard cases to a stronger model controls cost."),
    S("ev032", "eval", "An eval shows 12% failures. What should the team do first to decide what to fix?", ["Rewrite the whole prompt from scratch", "Switch to a different model", "Add a new rule to the prompt for each failing case you find", "Categorise failures by root cause, such as retrieval or format"], 3, "Grouping failures by cause shows where effort pays off. Rules per case tend to create conflicts without fixing a pattern."),
    S("ev033", "eval", "Structured output fails to parse in a small share of calls, and the broken ones end mid-object. Which check best diagnoses the cause?", ["Review the sampling temperature used for these requests", "Re-run the same inputs on a different day to compare", "Count the few-shot examples included in the prompt", "Check stop_reason for max_tokens truncation"], 3, "A response that reaches max_tokens is cut off, possibly mid-structure. The stop reason shows directly whether that happened."),
    S("ev034", "eval", "The same prompt gives inconsistent answers across runs on a factual task. Which technique helps flag unreliable answers?", ["Raise max_tokens so the answers have room to be more careful", "Run it several times and compare the outputs", "Add a polite greeting to the system prompt", "Lengthen the system prompt with general advice"], 1, "Best-of-N comparison is a documented check: inconsistency across runs can indicate hallucination."),
    S("ev035", "eval", "Failures cluster on questions where two clauses conflict, and the assistant confidently picks one. Which change addresses this?", ["Raise max_tokens so answers have room to discuss both conflicting clauses at length", "Forbid refusals and hedging so the assistant always commits firmly to one clause", "Have it quote both clauses and flag the conflict, then add conflict cases to the eval set", "Cache the system prompt and the retrieved clauses to keep answers more stable"], 2, "The failure is an unhandled case. Explicit instructions plus eval coverage make the behaviour correct and keep it tested.", "scEV1"),
    S("ev036", "eval", "A prompt with 25 rules produces inconsistent behaviour. Which diagnostic approach is best?", ["Add or remove rules in controlled steps and rerun the eval set", "Add more rules to cover the inconsistencies", "Ask users which of the rules they prefer to keep", "Move all rules to the end of the prompt and the examples to the start"], 0, "Controlled ablation on the eval set shows which instructions help, conflict or are ignored."),
    M("ev037", "eval", 2, "A summariser scores well on evals, yet users complain. Which TWO responses are sound?", ["Increase the context window to reduce complaints", "Treat the complaints as noise and keep the current metrics", "Check the rubric against human judgements of what users value", "Sample production inputs to check that the eval set reflects real traffic", "Assume the model changed overnight despite a pinned ID"], [2, 3], "A gap between offline scores and users usually means unrepresentative cases or a rubric that misses what matters. Both are checkable."),
    S("ev038", "eval", "An extraction task fails mainly on one document type with an unusual layout. What is the best first move?", ["Upgrade all traffic to the largest model available today", "Add a paragraph of general quality advice to the prompt", "Add examples of that document type, then re-measure", "Increase the batch size"], 2, "Targeted examples address a specific, evidenced gap at low cost. A model change should follow only if measurement shows it is needed."),
    S("ev039", "eval", "A request repeats several thousand tokens of fixed instructions and examples. Which optimisation most directly cuts cost and latency?", ["Cache the static prefix, placed first in the prompt", "Shuffle the examples on each call to vary the context", "Place the per-request data first so that it gets cached", "Raise max_tokens so that the answers can run longer"], 0, "Caching reuses a stable prefix. The prefix covers tools, system and messages in order, so static content must come first."),
    S("ev040", "eval", "What is the best first step toward lower cost without hurting quality?", ["Switch every step to the smallest model right away to maximise the savings on every ticket", "Cap max_tokens at a very small value for all steps to cut output cost as far as possible", "Delete the long prompt and rely on model defaults to cut input tokens on every request", "Test smaller models or lower effort per step on the labelled set, then route routine tickets"], 3, "Per-step evaluation on the labelled set shows where a smaller or cheaper configuration holds quality. Blanket cuts risk quality loss.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev041", "eval", "The nightly archive re-classification has no waiting user. Which change fits it best?", ["Stream results to a live dashboard", "Submit it through the Message Batches API", "Increase concurrency of synchronous calls", "Shorten the prompt and keep everything else the same"], 1, "Asynchronous batch processing suits bulk work where results are not needed immediately, and it is typically cheaper.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev042", "eval", "Agents need summaries within seconds of ticket arrival. Which approach and measures fit best?", ["Batch tickets and summarise once an hour to save cost", "Stream the summary and track both first-token and tail latency", "Wait until classification finishes for the whole queue", "Lengthen summaries so agents rarely need to open the ticket"], 1, "An interactive need calls for synchronous, streamed output measured by first-token time and tail latency.", "scEV2"),
    M("ev043", "eval", 2, "Which TWO changes reduce token use without changing the model?", ["Ask for concise output using sentence or paragraph limits", "Raise max_tokens so the model can say more", "Add extra examples to every request", "Repeat the instructions at both ends of the prompt", "Remove redundant text and unneeded context from the prompt"], [0, 4], "Concise instructions and trimmed context lower token counts. Sentence limits are more reliable than exact word counts."),
    S("ev044", "eval", "The team caches the long fixed prompt, but usage shows zero cache reads and zero cache writes. Which cause should be checked first?", ["The classification labels are too short to be eligible for caching", "The nightly job sends its requests with streaming turned on", "The labelled set is too small to trigger caching on the nightly run", "The cached prefix may be shorter than the minimum cacheable token length"], 3, "Prompts below the minimum are processed uncached without error, and both usage counters then read zero. Content that changes before the breakpoint also prevents hits.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev045", "eval", "A new feature has not yet reached its quality target, and the team wants it faster. What sequencing is best?", ["Reach target quality first, then apply latency measures", "Start with the smallest model and shortest output, and raise quality later", "Wait for user complaints about speed before measuring anything", "Skip quality work until the latency target is met"], 0, "Anthropic advises getting the prompt working well first, because premature optimisation can hide what top performance looks like."),
    S("ev046", "eval", "Moving classification to a smaller model loses accuracy on a small ambiguous slice. What is the best design decision?", ["Revert every ticket to the large model to restore accuracy on all slices", "Accept the loss on that slice, since cost is the priority for this project", "Route routine tickets to the smaller model and ambiguous ones to the larger model", "Add \"be accurate\" to the prompt for the smaller model and keep routing as is"], 2, "Routing by difficulty keeps savings on routine work and quality where it matters, and measurement validates the split.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev047", "eval", "A latency-sensitive extraction step is too slow. Which set matches Anthropic's documented latency guidance?", ["Use a faster model, trim input and output tokens, and stream the response", "Retry every request twice in parallel and keep whichever reply arrives first", "Send each request through the Message Batches API", "Maximise max_tokens so answers finish in one pass"], 0, "The latency guidance names model choice, prompt and output length, and streaming. Batching is asynchronous, so it does not reduce interactive latency."),
    S("ev048", "eval", "Which workload should NOT be moved to the Message Batches API?", ["Overnight scoring of an archive", "A large evaluation run over thousands of cases", "A live chat reply that a user is waiting on", "Bulk generation of catalogue descriptions"], 2, "Batches are asynchronous, so they suit work where results are not needed immediately, not interactive replies."),
    M("ev049", "eval", 3, "Which THREE are worth logging per request to support later diagnosis, subject to data-handling policy?", ["Raw personal data kept indefinitely for convenience", "The developer's IDE theme", "Prompt version and model ID", "Token usage, including cache read and write counts", "Only a final success flag, with nothing else", "stop_reason and error or retry status"], [2, 3, 5], "These fields let a team tie behaviour to releases and spot truncation, cost and failures. Indefinite raw personal data is a risk, and a bare success flag cannot support diagnosis."),
    S("ev050", "eval", "Which monitoring set best detects quality drift in the live ticket pipeline?", ["Server CPU utilisation only", "Sampled live outputs scored with the offline rubric, by category", "The monthly invoice total", "The count of tickets processed per day, plus the average time per ticket"], 1, "A successful response does not show quality. Scoring a sample with the offline rubric, by category, shows drift where it occurs.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev051", "eval", "Cost per ticket has risen sharply with unchanged traffic. Which data source best helps localise the cause?", ["The server uptime dashboard", "Browser console logs", "Customer survey results gathered after the end of each support conversation", "Usage reports by model, workspace or key, plus token logs"], 3, "Usage breakdowns plus per-request token data show which model, key or prompt change moved consumption."),
    S("ev052", "eval", "The team wants an alert before a prompt edit quietly doubles output length. What is best?", ["Review invoices quarterly", "Alert on HTTP 500 responses and timeouts from the service", "Read the vendor release notes each time a new model version ships", "Track output tokens by prompt version and alert on shifts"], 3, "Recording tokens per prompt version ties a shift to a release and surfaces it quickly. Invoices and error codes lag or miss it.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev053", "eval", "An agent calls several tools in a loop, and users report slow, wrong results. Which observability approach helps most?", ["Log only the final answers", "Trace each step, including tool calls, inputs and outputs", "Count requests per minute and chart the totals by hour of day", "Confirm that the API key is still valid"], 1, "Step-level traces show where time and errors arise in a multi-step run. Final answers or request counts hide that."),
    M("ev054", "eval", 2, "Which TWO are sound practices when logs may hold personal or regulated data?", ["Log everything verbatim forever, for later flexibility", "Assume technical logs are exempt from data protection rules", "Redact or minimise personal data and set a retention period agreed with privacy owners", "Copy logs to personal laptops for easier analysis", "Consult the Data Privacy Officer or Legal on what may be logged, where and for how long"], [2, 4], "Logging decisions involve data protection obligations, so minimisation and expert input are appropriate. Open-ended retention or uncontrolled copies are not."),
    S("ev055", "eval", "A dashboard shows stable average latency, yet agents complain about slow summaries at peak. What is the likeliest monitoring gap?", ["Cost per token is not tracked", "There is not enough labelled data to estimate how accurate the pipeline is", "Averages hide tail and peak-hour behaviour; track percentiles", "The prompt is too short"], 2, "Averages mask tail latency. Percentiles by time window, with queue and rate-limit signals, show peak-hour slowdowns.", "scEV2"),
    S("ev056", "eval", "Which alerting rule best balances detecting real regressions against alert fatigue?", ["Alert when a metric leaves its baseline band for a sustained window", "Alert on every single failed request", "Alert only after finance raises a complaint", "Alert on any change in daily ticket volume, however small or short-lived it is"], 0, "Sustained deviation from a baseline catches real regressions while ignoring isolated failures.", "scEV2")
  );

  Object.assign(window.CCA.why, {
    "ev001": ["An informal impression is not repeatable, and different reviewers will disagree, so it cannot show progress.", "Volume says nothing about whether the summaries are faithful or useful.", "A rubric-scored rate on a fixed set with a target is specific, measurable and repeatable across versions.", "Length is not a quality measure, and the longest summary is not a reference answer."],
    "ev002": ["Latency, cost and safety are common success dimensions, and accuracy alone can hide a problem in any of them.", "Usage frequency measures adoption, not whether answers are good, fast or safe.", "Public benchmarks do not measure your task, and your own evals should drive decisions.", "Authorship has no bearing on whether the system meets its criteria."],
    "ev003": ["Length is unrelated to the risk of unsupported citations.", "Satisfaction is a useful signal but does not detect fabricated citations, which users may not notice.", "First-token time is a latency measure and cannot gate a correctness risk.", "It measures the costliest failure directly, on cases the prompt was not tuned against."],
    "ev004": ["Without a threshold or measurement method, this cannot be tested or tracked.", "It names a rate, a detection method and a fixed prompt set, so the result is quantified and repeatable.", "A single informal session is a weak, unrepeatable sample.", "A prompt section is an implementation, not evidence that outputs are safe."],
    "ev005": ["Total time matters for non-interactive uses, but streaming users judge how soon text begins.", "Time to first token fits streaming, and a high percentile shows the experience of slower requests.", "A mean throughput figure hides startup delay and slow outliers.", "Client request building is a small part of delay and not what users perceive."],
    "ev006": ["Benign completion shows usefulness but nothing about resistance to malicious content.", "Cost is an efficiency dimension and does not measure attack resistance.", "Token counts describe consumption, not whether the agent can be steered.", "It measures the security risk directly, using adversarial cases containing injected instructions."],
    "ev007": ["Per-category results and added coverage expose the weak segment that the aggregate hides.", "Rare categories do not average away, and the failure persists for those users.", "A higher aggregate target does not reveal which segment is failing.", "Latency is a separate dimension and does not replace the correctness question."],
    "ev008": ["Flexible interpretation makes results inconsistent, so it is not a good property.", "A criterion tied to the specific task can be tested against it.", "A perfect-score target is unlikely to be achievable, and criteria should be realistic for current model capabilities.", "Measurable criteria can be tracked and compared across versions.", "Criteria defined after launch cannot guide design or release decisions."],
    "ev009": ["List price ignores how many tokens each configuration really uses.", "A pilot invoice mixes traffic differences and does not isolate the configuration.", "It includes prompt size, output length and retries, and compares like with like on the same set.", "Call counts do not reflect token volume or success."],
    "ev010": ["It reflects the real distribution and tests edge cases, so scores predict production behaviour.", "Clean-only examples overstate quality because real inputs are messier.", "Hard-only cases skew the picture and hide performance on typical traffic.", "Reusing prompt examples inflates results because the model has already seen them."],
    "ev011": ["Forty impressions do not support a rollout decision and have no references to score against.", "Unreviewed synthetic data may contain wrong references, so scores would be unreliable.", "Representative volume, edge cases and verified references make evidence the decision can rest on.", "Filtering to already-solved questions inflates the score and hides weaknesses."],
    "ev012": ["Human review is slow and costly where an exact check is possible.", "Exact match is the fastest, most reliable and most scalable method for fixed labels.", "Tone is irrelevant to whether the label is correct.", "Embedding similarity is imprecise when the output is one of a fixed label set."],
    "ev013": ["The answer will not match the passage exactly even when it is supported.", "Human grading of everything does not scale or fit a fast iteration cycle.", "A title keyword cannot show that the claim is supported.", "It brings nuance at scale, and validation against human labels confirms the grader is reliable."],
    "ev014": ["Without a rubric the grader's criteria are unclear and results vary more.", "Using the same model does not address the output-format problem.", "Longer critiques make aggregation harder, not easier.", "Detailed rubrics and constrained verdicts make grades consistent and easy to aggregate."],
    "ev015": ["Automated grading can be validated and calibrated, so rejecting it entirely limits volume needlessly.", "More cases at slightly lower signal beat fewer hand-graded ones, and spot checks keep grading honest.", "Random alternation produces results that cannot be compared between runs.", "Passing cases also show regressions and baseline performance, so ignoring them hides information."],
    "ev016": ["Latency is unrelated to the overfitting risk described.", "The issue is overfitting of the prompt to visible cases, not memorisation that needs fine-tuning.", "A held-out set shows whether the gain generalises beyond the cases used for tuning.", "Rate limits are unrelated to the reliability of the reported score."],
    "ev017": ["Full human grading after every edit is too slow for iteration.", "Code checks suit exact criteria and an LLM grader suits tone, so each criterion gets a fitting method.", "Public benchmarks do not represent your inputs or criteria.", "An unvalidated LLM grader can drift from what humans judge to be correct.", "Human samples calibrate automated graders without grading everything by hand."],
    "ev018": ["A regression suite turns each past failure into an automatic check on every change.", "A larger model may behave differently but does not stop prompt edits from reintroducing failures.", "A manual monthly check is too slow and inconsistent to catch regressions.", "Listing bugs in the prompt lengthens it and does not test whether they recur."],
    "ev019": ["Different weeks introduce time effects, so differences may come from traffic rather than the prompt.", "Self-selection bias means users who choose a variant differ from those who do not.", "Concurrent random assignment and a predefined metric control for time and selection effects.", "Unblinded reviewers are subject to bias toward the variant they expect to be better."],
    "ev020": ["Offline comparison is cheap and safe, and a canary limits exposure while evidence builds.", "Full cutover exposes every user to untested behaviour.", "Without metrics, a month of staff use produces opinions, not evidence.", "A model is not a reliable judge of its own prompt's effect."],
    "ev021": ["Changing three things at once prevents attributing any difference to chunking.", "Different question sets make the results incomparable.", "Memorable questions are a biased and tiny sample.", "Varying only chunking on a shared set isolates its effect."],
    "ev022": ["Two points on thirty cases may be noise, so rolling out may rest on chance.", "More cases or repeated runs give a basis for deciding whether the gain is real.", "Discarding a possibly real gain also decides on weak evidence.", "Averaging scores does not tell you which variant is better."],
    "ev023": ["Leaderboard rank does not measure behaviour on your traffic.", "Latency percentiles show whether the cheaper setup slows users, especially in the tail.", "The number of prompt edits describes activity, not the system's outcomes.", "Cost per task confirms that the cheaper setup actually saves money.", "Safety flag or escalation rates show whether quality or safety behaviour shifted.", "Team satisfaction is not an outcome measure of the configuration."],
    "ev024": ["Shipping ignores the harm from refusing answerable questions, which weakens usefulness.", "Quantifying both sides and tuning to targets treats the change as a trade-off to manage.", "Reverting throws away a real gain, when tuning may reach both targets.", "Removing cases hides the problem rather than fixing it."],
    "ev025": ["Unreported failures and new regressions also matter, so deploying on this basis is risky.", "Retesting only five cases cannot reveal regressions elsewhere.", "Waiting for users to report exposes them to the regressions first.", "A full rerun catches regressions, and adding the cases prevents the failures from returning."],
    "ev026": ["A small live share with agreed metrics gives production evidence at low risk.", "Offline evidence cannot cover every real input, so full cutover is riskier than needed.", "Traffic level is not a control for unknown differences.", "Starting with the hardest users concentrates risk rather than limiting it."],
    "ev027": ["Bundled changes prove nothing about which single change mattered.", "Nothing ensures that each change contributed, since some may have harmed.", "Without attribution, the team cannot reuse the good changes or drop the harmful ones.", "Latency can still be measured, so this is not the weakness."],
    "ev028": ["Checking what reached the context separates retrieval faults from generation faults and directs the fix.", "A bigger model will not fix missing content and may add cost with no benefit.", "Output length does not affect whether facts are grounded.", "Fewer examples do not address a grounding or retrieval problem."],
    "ev029": ["Citations make claims checkable, so removing them reduces auditability.", "Allowing general knowledge invites unsupported content when documents are silent.", "Extracting quotes first grounds the answer in the text, as Anthropic's guidance recommends.", "Longer answers give more room for errors, not fewer.", "Permitting the model to say it does not know reduces invented answers."],
    "ev030": ["Re-chunking changes retrieval, but the clause was not retrieved, so the issue is generation.", "More passages do not stop the model citing something that is not there.", "Exact quotes verified in code make unsupported citations detectable, so they can be rejected.", "A cheaper grader does not fix the assistant and may weaken detection."],
    "ev031": ["More iteration on a prompt that has barely moved is unlikely to help.", "Little gain after many changes on a hard slice points to a capability limit, so test a stronger model or route those cases.", "Temperature is unlikely to fix a reasoning shortfall.", "Piling on examples is unlikely to close a capability gap after many tries."],
    "ev032": ["A rewrite discards what works and does not locate the problem.", "Switching models may not address the actual cause.", "Rules per case can conflict and treat symptoms, not patterns.", "Categorising by cause shows which fix will have the largest effect."],
    "ev033": ["Sampling settings do not explain output that stops mid-object.", "Re-running on another day does not reveal a systematic cause.", "Example count rarely explains truncation.", "A max_tokens stop reason shows the output was cut off at the configured limit."],
    "ev034": ["Output length does not make answers more consistent.", "Repeating and comparing runs is a documented check: disagreement flags possible hallucination.", "A greeting does not affect factual reliability.", "Generic advice in the system prompt does not address inconsistency."],
    "ev035": ["Longer answers do not resolve a conflict between sources.", "Forcing commitment worsens confident wrong picks.", "Surfacing the conflict, flagging for review and adding eval cases fixes and tests the behaviour.", "Caching affects cost and latency, not this failure."],
    "ev036": ["Controlled steps reveal which rules help, conflict or are ignored.", "More rules usually add further conflicts.", "User preference does not show how the model treats the rules.", "Moving rules changes position but does not diagnose a conflict."],
    "ev037": ["A larger context window does not address mismatched evals.", "Dismissing complaints leaves a probable eval gap unaddressed.", "Comparing the rubric with human judgements checks whether it measures what users value.", "Sampling production inputs tests whether the eval set matches real use.", "A pinned model ID fixes the model, so this explanation is unlikely."],
    "ev038": ["Upgrading everything is costly and unproven when the failure is confined to one type.", "General advice is untargeted and unlikely to fix a specific gap.", "Targeted examples address the evidenced gap, and measuring afterward shows if a model change is still needed.", "Batch size does not affect extraction accuracy on a layout."],
    "ev039": ["Caching reuses a stable prefix, so static content first lets later calls hit it.", "Shuffling examples changes the prefix each call and prevents cache hits.", "Per-request data first makes the prefix differ on every call, so nothing matches.", "A higher output limit does not reduce input cost or time."],
    "ev040": ["Replacing every model at once risks quality losses that nobody has measured.", "A very small cap truncates outputs and does not measure which steps tolerate a cheaper model.", "Removing the prompt discards instructions that quality depends on.", "Testing per-step options on the labelled set finds savings that hold quality."],
    "ev041": ["Streaming helps interactive users, but nobody is waiting on this job.", "Without a waiting user, an asynchronous batch is the fit and is typically cheaper.", "More synchronous concurrency adds load without matching the workload.", "A shorter prompt helps a little, but does not use the batch pattern."],
    "ev042": ["Hourly batching misses the few-second requirement.", "Streaming and first-token and tail-latency measures fit an interactive need.", "Waiting for the whole queue delays every summary.", "Length does not address the latency requirement."],
    "ev043": ["Sentence or paragraph limits reduce output tokens reliably.", "A higher output cap can increase tokens.", "More examples in each request add input tokens.", "Repetition adds input tokens without benefit.", "Removing redundant text and unneeded context cuts input tokens."],
    "ev044": ["Label length does not control whether caching occurs.", "Streaming does not prevent caching.", "The size of the labelled set is unrelated to caching.", "Below the minimum length, requests are processed uncached with no error, and both counters then read zero."],
    "ev045": ["Establishing quality first shows what top performance looks like before trading anything away.", "Starting small can hide what good output looks like and wastes iteration.", "Waiting for complaints delays measurement that should guide the design.", "Skipping quality leaves no basis for judging whether the latency gains are acceptable."],
    "ev046": ["Reverting everything gives up savings available on routine tickets.", "Accepting avoidable losses on ambiguous tickets may harm outcomes that matter most.", "Routing by difficulty keeps savings on routine tickets and quality where needed, and the split should be measured.", "A one-line instruction does not close a capability gap."],
    "ev047": ["Model choice, shorter prompts and outputs, and streaming are the levers the latency guidance names.", "Duplicate requests add load and cost and do not reduce latency.", "Batches are asynchronous and not intended for interactive latency.", "A larger output cap does not speed responses."],
    "ev048": ["Overnight archive scoring has no waiting user, so batching fits it.", "Large evaluation runs are a documented batch use case.", "A user is waiting, so asynchronous batching is the wrong fit.", "Bulk generation is a documented batch use case."],
    "ev049": ["Retaining raw personal data indefinitely creates privacy risk and should be agreed with privacy owners.", "An IDE theme has no diagnostic value.", "Versions tie behaviour changes to releases.", "Token and cache counts support cost and caching diagnosis.", "A bare success flag cannot explain why failures occur.", "Stop reasons and error status reveal truncation and failures."],
    "ev050": ["CPU use shows system health but not whether answers are good.", "Scoring a sample by category with the offline rubric reveals quality drift where it happens.", "An invoice total is too coarse and too late to show quality problems.", "Ticket counts show volume, not correctness."],
    "ev051": ["Uptime shows availability, not what changed in consumption.", "Browser logs are unrelated to server-side API cost.", "Survey results do not explain token consumption.", "Breakdowns by model, workspace or key with token logs localise which component changed."],
    "ev052": ["Quarterly invoices lag far behind the change.", "A longer output does not cause HTTP errors, so the alert would stay silent.", "Release notes describe vendor changes, not your prompt edit.", "Output tokens by prompt version link the shift to a release and alert quickly."],
    "ev053": ["Final answers alone cannot show which step was slow or wrong.", "Step-level traces show where time and errors occur in the loop.", "Request counts say nothing about step behaviour.", "A valid key does not explain slow or wrong results."],
    "ev054": ["Verbatim logging forever raises privacy and compliance exposure.", "Logs holding personal data are generally within scope of data protection rules.", "Minimising and redacting data with an agreed retention period reduces risk.", "Copies on personal laptops escape access controls.", "The DPO or Legal should decide logging scope and retention for regulated data."],
    "ev055": ["Cost tracking does not explain a latency complaint.", "Labelled data size does not explain peak-hour slowness.", "Averages hide tails and peaks, so percentiles by time with queue and rate-limit signals expose them.", "Prompt length is not what the stable average-versus-peak gap points to."],
    "ev056": ["A sustained band breach signals a real regression without noise from isolated failures.", "Alerting on every failure creates fatigue and buries real signals.", "Waiting for finance means regressions run for a long time.", "Volume changes are often normal and do not signal quality problems."]
  });
})();
